-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+Eviction uses 2Q. Each cached sheet lives in one of two queues, plus a ghost list of recently evicted keys:
+
+- **A1in** (FIFO): a sheet that has been requested exactly once. New completions enter here.
+- **A1out** (ghost list, keys only): a sheet recently evicted from A1in. No JPEG data is retained; only the key, to detect reuse.
+- **Am** (LRU): a sheet that has been requested again — either while still in A1in, or after falling into A1out. Entry into Am marks a sheet as reused and moves it out of one-off eviction order.
+
+Eviction always drains A1in before touching Am, so a long sequential screening fills and empties A1in without displacing sheets editors are actively comparing in Am. The capacity split between A1in and Am is a tunable parameter; see Open Questions for its starting value and how it will be tuned.
+
+This replaces the previously open choice between LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q — see Decisions & Alternatives. None of the three candidates changed the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget; the choice is internal to this cache.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q (A1in/A1out/Am queues) | LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging | See [decisions/cache-eviction-policy.md](decisions/cache-eviction-policy.md). |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Tune the A1in/Am capacity split (and the A1out ghost-list size) against real pilot traffic. Start from the conventional 2Q split — A1in sized at roughly one quarter of the 12 GiB budget, Am the remainder, A1out tracking a comparable number of keys to A1in — and adjust once screening/comparison mixes from the pilot are observed.
